1.text:
In air the charge which is collected in the ion chamber is

\begin{equation*}
I=\frac{e}{W}\,\dot E_{abs}\qquad 
\dot E_{abs}= \dot K_{air}\,m_{air}
\end{equation*}

so that with $W/e=33.97\;{\rm V}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{I}{m_{air}}\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}
is the air kerma rate in the illuminated part of the chamber.  The
illuminated air mass at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{ill}
       =\rho_{air} \,A_{b} \,L_{c}\;,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{b}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},
\qquad L_{c}=15.1\;{\rm cm}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{ill}=A_{b}L_{c}=2.72\times10^{-1}\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\qquad 
m_{air}=1.293\,{\rm mg\,cm^{-3}}V_{ill}=3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
       =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}\;.
\end{equation*}

Hence on the assumption that the $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ quoted is the
instantaneous current when the chamber is completely illuminated

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= 
\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\ {\rm A}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}}\,
33.97\ {\rm J\,C^{-1}}
\approx 1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in air at the focus, viz. $\simeq 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$, in agreement
with

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \Phi \!E\,\mu_{en}/\rho\;,
\qquad 
\Phi=\frac{ I W}{e\,m_{air}E\,\mu_{en}/\rho}\approx
1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV, $\mu_{en}/\rho=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$.
For tissue $\mu_{en}/\rho\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho|_{air}$ so that the same
number is the tissue kerma rate in free space.

A tissue element which is carried through the stationary focus stays
in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal beam for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{\hbox{scan speed}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{(\Delta x/\Delta t)}\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}
the 0.02 s quoted in the text.  The kerma received by a surface
element in one passage, i.e. for one monoenergetic exposure, is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\approx\dot K_{air}\;t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx 3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$\,K_{1}\approx 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.39\ {\rm mrad}$ in free air
and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$, the same
$\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ surface kerma to tissue for a single
33 keV exposure.  This is the familiar $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
air-kerma rate at the 0.3$\times$6 ${\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.

For the usual dual-energy – or more generally $N_{E}$–energy –
synchrotron multiple-energy acquisition the surface kerma is $N_{E}$
times this, e.g. $N_{E}=2$

\[
K_{2}\approx2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
0.008\;{\rm mGy}
\]

per resolved surface element, i.e. of order $10^{-6}\!-\!10^{-5}{\rm Gy}$
at the patient surface, essentially the $0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ free-in-air
kerma rate at the focus quoted above.  The 0.02 s dwell time is the
effective exposure time for any one point of the scanned field, so the
dose to a given point remains $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monoenergetic
pass.  This is the $\sim 10^{-6}\,$Gy level normally quoted for
synchrotron phase–contrast / multi-energy scanning micro–CT.

6.text:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge which is collected per unit time
is

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q=I\;\;\;{\rm C\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\dot N_{i}=\frac{I}{e}\;{\rm ion\;pairs\;s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

so that the power which is deposited in the air which is actually
irradiated is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\dot N_{i}W=\frac{W}{e}I
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq33.97\,{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ for air.  With the $2.0{\rm \;pA}$
reading

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{33.97}{1}\times2.0\times10^{-12}\approx 
6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is deposited in the volume which the beam actually sees

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=A_{f}\ell ,\qquad
A_{f}=0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}},\;\;
\ell=15.1\;{\rm cm}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=0.2718\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{b}=\rho_{a}V_{b}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.2718\;{\rm
cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}\approx3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}.
\end{equation*}

Hence the air-kerma rate at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot k_{a}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{b}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{b}}
      \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
      \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $\mu_{en}/\rho\approx0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}\,g^{-1}}$ at
33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot k_{a}= \Phi\,E\,\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho},
\qquad
\Phi\approx\frac{\dot k_{a}}{E\,\mu_{en}/\rho}\sim1\times10^{8}\;
{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus, the well-known $W/e$ result being recovered.  For tissue
$\mu_{en}/\rho\approx\mu_{en}/\rho|_{a}$ the absorbed dose in tissue is
the same as the air kerma to a good approximation.

A point of the sample which is translated with the scan speed $v$ stays
in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{p}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}=0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

per energy frame.  The dose received in one energy frame is therefore

\begin{equation*}
k_{1}= \dot k_{a}\,t_{p}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.9\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per point per energy, i.e. $\sim0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\,{\rm ms}$,
the conventional “single-energy’’ surface dose at the focus.  In
cgs

\begin{equation*}
k_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-7}\;{\rm J\,g^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

which is the dose that would be quoted for one monochromator setting.

In a Laue bent-crystal multi-energy scan the $w_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$
horizontal illumination of the crystal is demagnified to the
$w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focal spot.  The number of essentially
independent energies which can be accessed in one translation of the
sample is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{w_{0}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

the vertical size being unchanged.  The total point dose accumulated
on a given surface element when the whole energy fan is acquired is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{cum}\approx N_{E}k_{1}
       \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.87\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. of the order $0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ per surface point for the
complete multi-energy data set, $\approx 0.65\;{\rm mGy}\approx
645\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ with the numbers quoted.  This is $N_{E}$ times the
$ \approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ single-energy frame dose quoted above.
If the dose is quoted per single monochromator setting the familiar
$ k_{1}\simeq4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms
residence, $\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma rate at the focus,
is obtained.  The $\,0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ figure is the customary
cumulative multi-energy surface dose, essentially $ \sim 64\;{\rm mrad}$
to the tissue surface on the assumption $\mu_{en}/\rho|_{t}\approx
\mu_{en}/\rho|_{a}$.

20.text:
For an air filled ionisation chamber the energy put into the air in the
beam per unit time is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,\frac{W}{e}\simeq I\times 33.97\;{\rm V}\;
\end{equation*}

in SI units.  With $W/e\approx 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm
W}
\end{equation*}

is the power which is put into the air which is actually traversed by
the beam.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with a geometrical depth of the chamber in the beam of
$L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L =0.018\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

is the air volume which is irradiated at any instant.  With
$\rho_{air}=1.293\;{\rm mg\,cm^{-3}}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx
3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air in the beam.  The air kerma rate at the focus is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{air}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\approx
1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 0.7\;{\rm mGy\,h^{-1}}$ air kerma at the focus.  With
$\left(\mu_{en}/\rho\right)_{tissue}\simeq\left(\mu_{en}/\rho\right)_{air}
=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}\,g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the same number is the tissue
kerma rate in the beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the 0.30 mm $\times$ 6.0 mm focus.

A material point on the sample is in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{e}}
\end{equation*}

where $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ is the horizontal width of the focus and
$v_{s}$ the scan speed.  In the notation used in the paper
$t_{d}=w_{f}/(\Delta x/\Delta t_{e})\approx2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}$ is
the dwell of a point in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ beam during one energy
exposure.  The kerma delivered to that point in one energy exposure is
hence

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, i.e. about $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell at the
surface in the $0.30\;{\rm mm}\times 6\;{\rm mm}$ focus.  In the
conventional dual-energy implementation the surface kerma is essentially
doubled,

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\approx2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}\approx
8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, essentially $4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, about
$0.7\;{\rm mGy\,h^{-1}}$ in the beam.  This is the peak surface kerma in
the beam.

If the $0.02$ quoted in the statement is taken as the dimensionless
overlap $w_{f}/\Delta x=0.02$, the mean surface kerma averaged over the
scanned field is $K_{av}=K_{1}w_{f}/\Delta x$.  With an exposure time
per step $\Delta t_{e}=0.02\;{\rm s}$ the mean dwell per point is
$t_{d}^{av}=\Delta t_{e}w_{f}/\Delta x=4.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm s}$ and

\begin{equation*}
K_{av}\approx\dot K_{t}\Delta t_{e}\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x}
\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times4.0\times10^{-4}\approx7.7\times10^{-8}\;
{\rm Gy}\approx 80\;{\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, $\sim1.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional dual
energy acquisition, i.e. $ \sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ peak per energy, $\sim
 77\,{\rm nGy}$ mean over the scanned area, which is the usual way the
number is quoted in the literature.  The $3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ peak value
is $ \approx 7.7\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a two-energy acquisition and is the
surface dose normally quoted for the synchrotron multi-energy micro-scan.

11.text:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge collected per unit time is

* $I=e\dot N_{i}$  $N_{i}$ the number of ion pairs created per second and
* $\dot E_{abs}= \dot N_{i}W = I\,W/e$

is the energy which is stopped in the air of the chamber per second.  With
the usual value $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=I\,W/e =2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
          =6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is deposited in the air which is actually in the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times 6.0{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the quoted chamber depth $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=2.72\times 10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{f}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
        \times2.718\times10^{-1}{\rm cm^{3}}
     =3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant.
Hence the air kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{abs}}{m_{f}}
          =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{f}}
          \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$,
the same kerma rate in soft tissue.

For a point which is carried through the beam with the scan speed
$v_{s}={\rm step}/{\rm exposure}$ the beam dwell time is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\rm step/exposure}
\end{equation*}

which is the quantity quoted in the statement.  With the quoted
value

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the kerma received by a single material point in one energy exposure is

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{air}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times
10^{-2}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy dwell, essentially the
surface air kerma at the focus.  In conventional units

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\approx
3.9\ {\rm \mu Gy\ at\ 20\,ms\; dwell}
\end{equation*}

per energy channel.  This is the dose which a given piece of tissue
sees while it is traversed by the $0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ beam
for the $t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\approx20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell.

If the whole Laue band which is incident on the crystal is used, the
50 mm horizontal aperture at the crystal is demagnified to $0.30\;{\rm
mm}$ at the focus.  The number of essentially independent energy
channels which can be put into the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\approx\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\;,
\end{equation*}

so that the integrated kerma which is deposited in a point which is
sampled over the whole Laue band is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\approx N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete multiple-energy scan, i.e. of the order of a sub-milligray
surface exposure, essentially $ \sim0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ for the nominal
$~167$ energy positions.  The $ \sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per single energy
dwell is the conventionally quoted ``per-image'' surface kerma.  This
is the $\sim 10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ air-kerma rate often quoted for
a $33\,{\rm keV}$ synchrotron Laue micro-beam.

18.text:
In an air filled chamber the charge which is collected per unit mass of air
is the exposure

\begin{equation*}
\dot X=\frac{I}{m_{air}}\qquad {\rm C\,kg^{-1}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

and with the definition $D_{air}=X\,W/e$ the air kerma rate under
electronic equilibrium is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}= \frac{I}{m_{air}}\frac{W}{e}\approx
\frac{I}{m_{air}}\,33.97\;\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}\;,
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ for air.  The air which is actually
irradiated at the focus is the beam volume

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;
{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times 15.1=2.72\times 10^{-1}\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=\rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
\times 2.72\times10^{-1}\;{\rm cm^{3}}\approx 3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

Hence on the beam axis

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}= \frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\;{\rm A}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\;{\rm
kg}}\,33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}\approx 1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma at the focus, essentially the
absorbed dose in the air.  For soft tissue $\mu_{en}/\rho \simeq
\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$ at 33 keV so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{tissue}\approx\dot D_{air}\approx 2\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\;s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

while the beam is on the point.  

For a one dimensional raster the time a material point spends in the
horizontal beam is the geometrical dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}\approx
\frac{w_{f}\;{\rm per\;scan\;step\;per\;exposure\;time}}{}
\end{equation*}

which is the quoted

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\simeq 2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

for the 0.30 mm wide focus.  The dose actually received on a first pass
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{air}\;t_{d}
\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
\approx3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\sim 3$–$4\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ of
air kerma, and with $\mu_{en}/\rho_{tissue}\approx\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$
$\approx$ $3$–$4\,\mu$Gy absorbed in soft tissue per mono-energetic
transit of the point through the 0.30 mm $\times$ 6 mm focus.  This is
the nominal surface air kerma per energy.  For an $N_{E}$ energy
multiple-energy acquisition the nominal surface dose scales as
$N_{E}D_{1}$, e.g. $\approx 1.2\times10^{-5}\,$Gy for a typical
$N_{E}=3$ set, $\approx 0.012\,$mGy in total, essentially $10^{-5}$–$10^{-6}$
Gy per pass.  In the often quoted mGy units this is $\approx 0.004$
mGy per mono-energetic pass, $\approx 0.01$–$0.02$ mGy for a three energy
synchrotron multiple-energy image, which is the order of magnitude
commonly quoted for the bent-Laue synchrotron micro-beam work.  If the
average dose over the whole rastered field is required the average is
$D_{1}$ multiplied by the duty factor $w_{f}/\Delta x$, which for the
nominal $t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}$ is the number quoted above.

3.text:
In an air filled chamber

* $W/e\simeq 33.9\,{\rm V}$  is the mean energy per ion pair
* $I=\dot Q=e\dot N_{ip}$

so that the power which is absorbed in the air which is intersected by
the beam is

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}=\dot N_{ip}W=\frac{I\,W}{e}
     =I\frac{W}{e}\;,
\end{equation*}

and for a mono-energetic beam

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}=\dot\Phi_{E}\, \mu_{en}\rho\, m
    =\dot\Phi_{E}\,\mu_{en/\rho}\,m ,
\end{equation*}

where $\dot\Phi_{E}$ is the energy fluence rate incident on the beam
area.  The air kerma, which for the low linear attenuation which is
present here is essentially the absorbed dose in air, is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{P_{a}}{m}
           =\frac{I}{e m}\frac{W}{e}\; ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the mass energy absorption coefficient cancels in the dose.

The volume of air which is actually irradiated at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
V =A_{f}\,L
  =\bigl(w_{f}h_{f}\bigr)L ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018~{\rm cm^{2}}, \qquad
L=15.1~{\rm cm},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V=0.2718~{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m=\rho_{air}V =1.293\times10^{-3}~{\rm g~cm^{-3}}\times0.2718~{\rm cm^{3}}
   \approx3.5\times10^{-4}~{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}~{\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

and with $I=2.0\,{\rm pA}$

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.9\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}.
\end{equation*}

Hence the dose rate in air at the geometric focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}=\frac{P_{a}}{m}
          \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}~{\rm J\,s^{-1}}}
                       {3.5\times10^{-7}~{\rm kg}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in the $33~{\rm keV}$ beam.  With $\mu_{en}/\rho_{tissue}\simeq
\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$ the absorbed dose in soft tissue may be taken to
be the same as the air kerma to the first order.

A point of the tissue which is being scanned transversely to the beam
is illuminated for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}

i.e. the ratio which is quoted in the statement,

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \dfrac{w_{f}}{\,{\rm scan~step/\,exposure~time}\,}\simeq0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that the dose delivered to a surface element during the time it
lies in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{air}\;t_{d}
     \approx 1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
     \approx 3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic projection at 33.0 keV, i.e. of order a few
$\mu{\rm Gy}$ at the surface.  With the conventional $W_{air}=33.97\,{\rm
eV}$,

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\frac{I\,W_{air}}{e\,m}\,t_{d}
       \approx3.9\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell, about $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,h^{-1}}$ air kerma
rate at the focus.  This is the dose that a given surface element
receives while the focal spot passes over it once.  For a multi-energy
protocol the dose adds linearly, $D_{N}\approx N D_{1}$, e.g. $\approx
7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional dual-energy acquisition, and
$N\,D_{1}$ in general.  The numbers quoted are for one 33 keV
projection; the incident $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ beam–$0.30\,{\rm
mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ focus demagnification $ \simeq 1.7\times10^{2}$
is already embodied in the measured $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ focal current. ∎

8.text:
For the air in the beam the energy which is absorbed per unit time is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

with $W\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV}$ the $W$ value of air.  For the quoted
current

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times 33.97\;{\rm V}
        =6.8\times 10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is absorbed in the air which is traversed by the beam.  With the
beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times 6.0{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and the air mass which is traversed in the $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ long
chamber

\begin{equation*}
m_{\rm a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
       =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.8\times10^{-2}\!
        {\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1{\rm cm}=3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g},
\end{equation*}

the air–kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\simeq
        1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{{\rm
air}}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ the same number is the kerma rate in
soft tissue at the surface in the beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\Phi_{E}\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}
     {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\Phi_{E}\simeq5.9\times10^{-7}{\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ or
$N\!\simeq1.1\times10^{8}{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ at 33 keV in the
$0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  The local dose which a surface
element receives while it is covered by the micro-beam is

\begin{equation*}
K_{t}= \dot K_{t}\,\Delta t_{p},
\qquad 
\Delta t_{p}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}} ,
\end{equation*}

$w_{f}=0.30$ mm the beam width at the focus and $v_{s}= \Delta x/t_{e}$
the scan speed.  The quantity

\begin{equation*}
\Delta t_{p}= \frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}
\end{equation*}

is quoted as $\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\,$s for the present scan.  Hence
on a $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell

\begin{equation*}
K_{t}^{p}\approx\dot K_{t}\,\Delta t_{p}
      \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
      \approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

is deposited in the surface element which is instantaneously under the
beam, i.e. $\,\approx 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy step, $\approx
0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per projection, $ \dot K_{t}\approx2\times10^{-4}\,
{\rm Gy\,min^{-1}}$ locally in the beam.  This is the customary peak
surface kerma quoted for the 33 keV micro-beam.  For a conventional
2–3 energy multi-energy acquisition the total peak surface dose is
$ \sim 8\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy} $.

If the dose is referred to the nominal $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
field which is scanned with the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus the mean surface
kerma averaged over the nominal field is

\begin{equation*}
\bar K_{t}=K_{t}^{p}\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}
          \approx \frac{3.9\times10^{-6}}{50/0.30}\approx
          2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per projection, $\approx 23\;{\rm nGy}$ per 20 ms step, $\approx
1.2\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy\,min^{-1}}$ mean over the 50 mm field, the
usual way the "cumulative" mean surface dose of the scanned image is
quoted.  The $3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection is the local peak
surface dose which is normally quoted for the synchrotron micro-beam;
the often quoted $\sim 10^{-8}\,$Gy mean value is the $w_{f}/W_{0}=0.006$
demagnified average over the 50 mm nominal field.

public_text.1:
In air the charge which is collected in the ion chamber is

\begin{equation*}
I=\frac{e}{W}\,\dot E_{abs}\qquad 
\dot E_{abs}= \dot K_{air}\,m_{air}
\end{equation*}

so that with $W/e=33.97\;{\rm V}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{I}{m_{air}}\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}
is the air kerma rate in the illuminated part of the chamber.  The
illuminated air mass at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{ill}
       =\rho_{air} \,A_{b} \,L_{c}\;,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{b}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},
\qquad L_{c}=15.1\;{\rm cm}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{ill}=A_{b}L_{c}=2.72\times10^{-1}\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\qquad 
m_{air}=1.293\,{\rm mg\,cm^{-3}}V_{ill}=3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
       =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}\;.
\end{equation*}

Hence on the assumption that the $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ quoted is the
instantaneous current when the chamber is completely illuminated

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= 
\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\ {\rm A}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}}\,
33.97\ {\rm J\,C^{-1}}
\approx 1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in air at the focus, viz. $\simeq 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$, in agreement
with

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \Phi \!E\,\mu_{en}/\rho\;,
\qquad 
\Phi=\frac{ I W}{e\,m_{air}E\,\mu_{en}/\rho}\approx
1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV, $\mu_{en}/\rho=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$.
For tissue $\mu_{en}/\rho\simeq\mu_{en}/\rho|_{air}$ so that the same
number is the tissue kerma rate in free space.

A tissue element which is carried through the stationary focus stays
in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal beam for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{\hbox{scan speed}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{(\Delta x/\Delta t)}\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}
the 0.02 s quoted in the text.  The kerma received by a surface
element in one passage, i.e. for one monoenergetic exposure, is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\approx\dot K_{air}\;t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx 3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$\,K_{1}\approx 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.39\ {\rm mrad}$ in free air
and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$, the same
$\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ surface kerma to tissue for a single
33 keV exposure.  This is the familiar $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
air-kerma rate at the 0.3$\times$6 ${\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.

For the usual dual-energy – or more generally $N_{E}$–energy –
synchrotron multiple-energy acquisition the surface kerma is $N_{E}$
times this, e.g. $N_{E}=2$

\[
K_{2}\approx2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
0.008\;{\rm mGy}
\]

per resolved surface element, i.e. of order $10^{-6}\!-\!10^{-5}{\rm Gy}$
at the patient surface, essentially the $0.2\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ free-in-air
kerma rate at the focus quoted above.  The 0.02 s dwell time is the
effective exposure time for any one point of the scanned field, so the
dose to a given point remains $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monoenergetic
pass.  This is the $\sim 10^{-6}\,$Gy level normally quoted for
synchrotron phase–contrast / multi-energy scanning micro–CT.

public_text.6:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge which is collected per unit time
is

\begin{equation*}
\dot Q=I\;\;\;{\rm C\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\dot N_{i}=\frac{I}{e}\;{\rm ion\;pairs\;s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

so that the power which is deposited in the air which is actually
irradiated is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\dot N_{i}W=\frac{W}{e}I
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq33.97\,{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ for air.  With the $2.0{\rm \;pA}$
reading

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{33.97}{1}\times2.0\times10^{-12}\approx 
6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is deposited in the volume which the beam actually sees

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=A_{f}\ell ,\qquad
A_{f}=0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}},\;\;
\ell=15.1\;{\rm cm}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=0.2718\;{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{b}=\rho_{a}V_{b}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.2718\;{\rm
cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}\approx3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}.
\end{equation*}

Hence the air-kerma rate at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot k_{a}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{b}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{b}}
      \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
      \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $\mu_{en}/\rho\approx0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}\,g^{-1}}$ at
33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot k_{a}= \Phi\,E\,\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho},
\qquad
\Phi\approx\frac{\dot k_{a}}{E\,\mu_{en}/\rho}\sim1\times10^{8}\;
{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at the focus, the well-known $W/e$ result being recovered.  For tissue
$\mu_{en}/\rho\approx\mu_{en}/\rho|_{a}$ the absorbed dose in tissue is
the same as the air kerma to a good approximation.

A point of the sample which is translated with the scan speed $v$ stays
in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{p}= \frac{w_{f}}{v}=0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

per energy frame.  The dose received in one energy frame is therefore

\begin{equation*}
k_{1}= \dot k_{a}\,t_{p}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.9\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per point per energy, i.e. $\sim0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\,{\rm ms}$,
the conventional “single-energy’’ surface dose at the focus.  In
cgs

\begin{equation*}
k_{1}\approx3.9\times10^{-7}\;{\rm J\,g^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

which is the dose that would be quoted for one monochromator setting.

In a Laue bent-crystal multi-energy scan the $w_{0}=50\;{\rm mm}$
horizontal illumination of the crystal is demagnified to the
$w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focal spot.  The number of essentially
independent energies which can be accessed in one translation of the
sample is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{w_{0}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

the vertical size being unchanged.  The total point dose accumulated
on a given surface element when the whole energy fan is acquired is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{cum}\approx N_{E}k_{1}
       \approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.87\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
       \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. of the order $0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ per surface point for the
complete multi-energy data set, $\approx 0.65\;{\rm mGy}\approx
645\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ with the numbers quoted.  This is $N_{E}$ times the
$ \approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ single-energy frame dose quoted above.
If the dose is quoted per single monochromator setting the familiar
$ k_{1}\simeq4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms
residence, $\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma rate at the focus,
is obtained.  The $\,0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ figure is the customary
cumulative multi-energy surface dose, essentially $ \sim 64\;{\rm mrad}$
to the tissue surface on the assumption $\mu_{en}/\rho|_{t}\approx
\mu_{en}/\rho|_{a}$.

public_text.20:
For an air filled ionisation chamber the energy put into the air in the
beam per unit time is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,\frac{W}{e}\simeq I\times 33.97\;{\rm V}\;
\end{equation*}

in SI units.  With $W/e\approx 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm
W}
\end{equation*}

is the power which is put into the air which is actually traversed by
the beam.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

so that with a geometrical depth of the chamber in the beam of
$L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L =0.018\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

is the air volume which is irradiated at any instant.  With
$\rho_{air}=1.293\;{\rm mg\,cm^{-3}}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx
3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air in the beam.  The air kerma rate at the focus is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{air}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\approx
1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 0.7\;{\rm mGy\,h^{-1}}$ air kerma at the focus.  With
$\left(\mu_{en}/\rho\right)_{tissue}\simeq\left(\mu_{en}/\rho\right)_{air}
=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}\,g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the same number is the tissue
kerma rate in the beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the 0.30 mm $\times$ 6.0 mm focus.

A material point on the sample is in the beam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{e}}
\end{equation*}

where $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ is the horizontal width of the focus and
$v_{s}$ the scan speed.  In the notation used in the paper
$t_{d}=w_{f}/(\Delta x/\Delta t_{e})\approx2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}$ is
the dwell of a point in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ beam during one energy
exposure.  The kerma delivered to that point in one energy exposure is
hence

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, i.e. about $3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell at the
surface in the $0.30\;{\rm mm}\times 6\;{\rm mm}$ focus.  In the
conventional dual-energy implementation the surface kerma is essentially
doubled,

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\approx2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}\approx
8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, essentially $4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, about
$0.7\;{\rm mGy\,h^{-1}}$ in the beam.  This is the peak surface kerma in
the beam.

If the $0.02$ quoted in the statement is taken as the dimensionless
overlap $w_{f}/\Delta x=0.02$, the mean surface kerma averaged over the
scanned field is $K_{av}=K_{1}w_{f}/\Delta x$.  With an exposure time
per step $\Delta t_{e}=0.02\;{\rm s}$ the mean dwell per point is
$t_{d}^{av}=\Delta t_{e}w_{f}/\Delta x=4.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm s}$ and

\begin{equation*}
K_{av}\approx\dot K_{t}\Delta t_{e}\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x}
\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times4.0\times10^{-4}\approx7.7\times10^{-8}\;
{\rm Gy}\approx 80\;{\rm nGy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, $\sim1.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional dual
energy acquisition, i.e. $ \sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ peak per energy, $\sim
 77\,{\rm nGy}$ mean over the scanned area, which is the usual way the
number is quoted in the literature.  The $3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ peak value
is $ \approx 7.7\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a two-energy acquisition and is the
surface dose normally quoted for the synchrotron multi-energy micro-scan.

public_text.11:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge collected per unit time is

* $I=e\dot N_{i}$  $N_{i}$ the number of ion pairs created per second and
* $\dot E_{abs}= \dot N_{i}W = I\,W/e$

is the energy which is stopped in the air of the chamber per second.  With
the usual value $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=I\,W/e =2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
          =6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is deposited in the air which is actually in the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times 6.0{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and with the quoted chamber depth $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=2.72\times 10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{f}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
        \times2.718\times10^{-1}{\rm cm^{3}}
     =3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the beam at any instant.
Hence the air kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{abs}}{m_{f}}
          =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{f}}
          \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$,
the same kerma rate in soft tissue.

For a point which is carried through the beam with the scan speed
$v_{s}={\rm step}/{\rm exposure}$ the beam dwell time is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\rm step/exposure}
\end{equation*}

which is the quantity quoted in the statement.  With the quoted
value

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the kerma received by a single material point in one energy exposure is

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{air}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times
10^{-2}\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy dwell, essentially the
surface air kerma at the focus.  In conventional units

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\approx
3.9\ {\rm \mu Gy\ at\ 20\,ms\; dwell}
\end{equation*}

per energy channel.  This is the dose which a given piece of tissue
sees while it is traversed by the $0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ beam
for the $t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\approx20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell.

If the whole Laue band which is incident on the crystal is used, the
50 mm horizontal aperture at the crystal is demagnified to $0.30\;{\rm
mm}$ at the focus.  The number of essentially independent energy
channels which can be put into the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\approx\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}\approx\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\;,
\end{equation*}

so that the integrated kerma which is deposited in a point which is
sampled over the whole Laue band is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\approx N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete multiple-energy scan, i.e. of the order of a sub-milligray
surface exposure, essentially $ \sim0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ for the nominal
$~167$ energy positions.  The $ \sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per single energy
dwell is the conventionally quoted ``per-image'' surface kerma.  This
is the $\sim 10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ air-kerma rate often quoted for
a $33\,{\rm keV}$ synchrotron Laue micro-beam.

public_text.18:
In an air filled chamber the charge which is collected per unit mass of air
is the exposure

\begin{equation*}
\dot X=\frac{I}{m_{air}}\qquad {\rm C\,kg^{-1}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

and with the definition $D_{air}=X\,W/e$ the air kerma rate under
electronic equilibrium is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}= \frac{I}{m_{air}}\frac{W}{e}\approx
\frac{I}{m_{air}}\,33.97\;\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}\;,
\end{equation*}

$W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$ for air.  The air which is actually
irradiated at the focus is the beam volume

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\;
{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times 15.1=2.72\times 10^{-1}\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=\rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
\times 2.72\times10^{-1}\;{\rm cm^{3}}\approx 3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}\; .
\end{equation*}

Hence on the beam axis

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}= \frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\;{\rm A}}{3.52\times10^{-7}\;{\rm
kg}}\,33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}\approx 1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma at the focus, essentially the
absorbed dose in the air.  For soft tissue $\mu_{en}/\rho \simeq
\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$ at 33 keV so that

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{tissue}\approx\dot D_{air}\approx 2\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\;s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

while the beam is on the point.  

For a one dimensional raster the time a material point spends in the
horizontal beam is the geometrical dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}\approx
\frac{w_{f}\;{\rm per\;scan\;step\;per\;exposure\;time}}{}
\end{equation*}

which is the quoted

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\simeq 2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

for the 0.30 mm wide focus.  The dose actually received on a first pass
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{air}\;t_{d}
\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
\approx3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\sim 3$–$4\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$ of
air kerma, and with $\mu_{en}/\rho_{tissue}\approx\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$
$\approx$ $3$–$4\,\mu$Gy absorbed in soft tissue per mono-energetic
transit of the point through the 0.30 mm $\times$ 6 mm focus.  This is
the nominal surface air kerma per energy.  For an $N_{E}$ energy
multiple-energy acquisition the nominal surface dose scales as
$N_{E}D_{1}$, e.g. $\approx 1.2\times10^{-5}\,$Gy for a typical
$N_{E}=3$ set, $\approx 0.012\,$mGy in total, essentially $10^{-5}$–$10^{-6}$
Gy per pass.  In the often quoted mGy units this is $\approx 0.004$
mGy per mono-energetic pass, $\approx 0.01$–$0.02$ mGy for a three energy
synchrotron multiple-energy image, which is the order of magnitude
commonly quoted for the bent-Laue synchrotron micro-beam work.  If the
average dose over the whole rastered field is required the average is
$D_{1}$ multiplied by the duty factor $w_{f}/\Delta x$, which for the
nominal $t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}$ is the number quoted above.

public_text.3:
In an air filled chamber

* $W/e\simeq 33.9\,{\rm V}$  is the mean energy per ion pair
* $I=\dot Q=e\dot N_{ip}$

so that the power which is absorbed in the air which is intersected by
the beam is

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}=\dot N_{ip}W=\frac{I\,W}{e}
     =I\frac{W}{e}\;,
\end{equation*}

and for a mono-energetic beam

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}=\dot\Phi_{E}\, \mu_{en}\rho\, m
    =\dot\Phi_{E}\,\mu_{en/\rho}\,m ,
\end{equation*}

where $\dot\Phi_{E}$ is the energy fluence rate incident on the beam
area.  The air kerma, which for the low linear attenuation which is
present here is essentially the absorbed dose in air, is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{P_{a}}{m}
           =\frac{I}{e m}\frac{W}{e}\; ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the mass energy absorption coefficient cancels in the dose.

The volume of air which is actually irradiated at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
V =A_{f}\,L
  =\bigl(w_{f}h_{f}\bigr)L ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018~{\rm cm^{2}}, \qquad
L=15.1~{\rm cm},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V=0.2718~{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m=\rho_{air}V =1.293\times10^{-3}~{\rm g~cm^{-3}}\times0.2718~{\rm cm^{3}}
   \approx3.5\times10^{-4}~{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}~{\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

and with $I=2.0\,{\rm pA}$

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.9\approx6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}.
\end{equation*}

Hence the dose rate in air at the geometric focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{air}=\frac{P_{a}}{m}
          \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}~{\rm J\,s^{-1}}}
                       {3.5\times10^{-7}~{\rm kg}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in the $33~{\rm keV}$ beam.  With $\mu_{en}/\rho_{tissue}\simeq
\mu_{en}/\rho_{air}$ the absorbed dose in soft tissue may be taken to
be the same as the air kerma to the first order.

A point of the tissue which is being scanned transversely to the beam
is illuminated for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v}
     =\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
\end{equation*}

i.e. the ratio which is quoted in the statement,

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \dfrac{w_{f}}{\,{\rm scan~step/\,exposure~time}\,}\simeq0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that the dose delivered to a surface element during the time it
lies in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{air}\;t_{d}
     \approx 1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
     \approx 3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic projection at 33.0 keV, i.e. of order a few
$\mu{\rm Gy}$ at the surface.  With the conventional $W_{air}=33.97\,{\rm
eV}$,

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\frac{I\,W_{air}}{e\,m}\,t_{d}
       \approx3.9\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $0.02\,{\rm s}$ dwell, about $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,h^{-1}}$ air kerma
rate at the focus.  This is the dose that a given surface element
receives while the focal spot passes over it once.  For a multi-energy
protocol the dose adds linearly, $D_{N}\approx N D_{1}$, e.g. $\approx
7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional dual-energy acquisition, and
$N\,D_{1}$ in general.  The numbers quoted are for one 33 keV
projection; the incident $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ beam–$0.30\,{\rm
mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ focus demagnification $ \simeq 1.7\times10^{2}$
is already embodied in the measured $2.0\;{\rm pA}$ focal current. ∎

public_text.8:
For the air in the beam the energy which is absorbed per unit time is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

with $W\simeq 33.97\;{\rm eV}$ the $W$ value of air.  For the quoted
current

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times 33.97\;{\rm V}
        =6.8\times 10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

is absorbed in the air which is traversed by the beam.  With the
beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times 6.0{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and the air mass which is traversed in the $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$ long
chamber

\begin{equation*}
m_{\rm a}= \rho_{a}A_{f}L
       =1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times1.8\times10^{-2}\!
        {\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1{\rm cm}=3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g},
\end{equation*}

the air–kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\simeq
        1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{\rm tissue}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{{\rm
air}}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ the same number is the kerma rate in
soft tissue at the surface in the beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\Phi_{E}\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}
     {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\Phi_{E}\simeq5.9\times10^{-7}{\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ or
$N\!\simeq1.1\times10^{8}{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ at 33 keV in the
$0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  The local dose which a surface
element receives while it is covered by the micro-beam is

\begin{equation*}
K_{t}= \dot K_{t}\,\Delta t_{p},
\qquad 
\Delta t_{p}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}} ,
\end{equation*}

$w_{f}=0.30$ mm the beam width at the focus and $v_{s}= \Delta x/t_{e}$
the scan speed.  The quantity

\begin{equation*}
\Delta t_{p}= \frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}
\end{equation*}

is quoted as $\approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\,$s for the present scan.  Hence
on a $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell

\begin{equation*}
K_{t}^{p}\approx\dot K_{t}\,\Delta t_{p}
      \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
      \approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

is deposited in the surface element which is instantaneously under the
beam, i.e. $\,\approx 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy step, $\approx
0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per projection, $ \dot K_{t}\approx2\times10^{-4}\,
{\rm Gy\,min^{-1}}$ locally in the beam.  This is the customary peak
surface kerma quoted for the 33 keV micro-beam.  For a conventional
2–3 energy multi-energy acquisition the total peak surface dose is
$ \sim 8\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy} $.

If the dose is referred to the nominal $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
field which is scanned with the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus the mean surface
kerma averaged over the nominal field is

\begin{equation*}
\bar K_{t}=K_{t}^{p}\frac{w_{f}}{W_{0}}
          \approx \frac{3.9\times10^{-6}}{50/0.30}\approx
          2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per projection, $\approx 23\;{\rm nGy}$ per 20 ms step, $\approx
1.2\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy\,min^{-1}}$ mean over the 50 mm field, the
usual way the "cumulative" mean surface dose of the scanned image is
quoted.  The $3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection is the local peak
surface dose which is normally quoted for the synchrotron micro-beam;
the often quoted $\sim 10^{-8}\,$Gy mean value is the $w_{f}/W_{0}=0.006$
demagnified average over the 50 mm nominal field.

3.text:
In an air chamber the charge collected per unit mass is the air exposure.
With $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=I\,\frac{W}{e},\qquad 
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{abs}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{air}A_{f}L}
\end{equation*}

is the air kerma rate in the air which is actually in the beam at the
focus.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
        \times0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
        =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
           \approx6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

so that the peak air kerma rate on the axis of the $0.30\times6\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
           \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}=0.328\;
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at $33\;{\rm keV}$ the same photon fluence gives

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

as the tissue kerma rate in free space in the beam.

A material point which is carried through the stationary focus stays in
the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal slit for the geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{exp}}
      \approx2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the number quoted in the statement.  The kerma accumulated in one
mono-energetic passage is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.004\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per 33 keV exposure per surface element, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
peak in the beam.  This is the $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection
normally quoted for the synchrotron Laue micro-beam.  For the usual
dual-energy material decomposition

\begin{equation*}
K_{2}\simeq2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, $ \sim10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}$ of essentially negligible
surface exposure.

In terms of the monochromator geometry the $50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal
illumination at the crystal is demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at
the focus so that the number of essentially independent Laue energies
which can be put into the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\;,
\end{equation*}

the vertical size being unchanged.  The integrated surface kerma of a
point which is sampled over the whole Laue band is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete multi-energy scan of the $50\;{\rm mm}\times6\;{\rm mm}$
field, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted.  The $K_{1}\approx
3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell is the conventional per-
energy, per-resolution-element surface dose; the $N_{E}K_{1}\sim0.6\;{\rm
mGy}$ figure is the cumulative exposure obtained when the whole
$50{\rm mm}$ Laue fan is integrated.  The $W/e$ conversion makes the
result independent of the quoted $\mu_{en}/\rho$, which is used only to
justify $\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}$. ∎

12.text:
The air kerma in the illuminated part of the chamber is obtained from the
ionisation current

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=I\,W/e\;, \qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air that is actually in the beam at the focus.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =0.03\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\,{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=\rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
2.72\times10^{-1}{\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that on the beam axis

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{abs}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}
           \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\dot K_{air}\simeq0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma at the
$0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the same rate is the tissue kerma rate in free
space, $\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}$.

A surface element which is translated through the stationary focus stays
in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal slit for the geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{exp}}\approx0.02\ {\rm s}\;,
\end{equation*}

the quantity quoted in the statement.  The kerma received in one
mono-energetic passage is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV dwell at the surface, $\approx
0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous in the focus.  In the usual
dual-energy implementation

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, the familiar $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy,
$\sim10^{-5}{\rm Gy}$ per dual-energy image, peak surface kerma.

The $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the Laue crystal is
demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ at the focus, so that the number
of essentially independent energy channels in the Laue fan is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}\simeq\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\; .
\end{equation*}

The dose which is summed over the whole fan for one surface point is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\,{\rm mGy}$ surface kerma per complete
multiple-energy scan, $\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted,
i.e. $\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak at the focus reduced to the
$\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ mono-energetic dwell, $\sim
7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional two-energy acquisition.  The
$\mu_{en}/\rho$ value cancels in the $I\to K$ conversion under electronic
equilibrium so the result is independent of the quoted $0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
to first order. ∎

15.text:
The air kerma which the chamber sees in the $0.30\times 6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus is
obtained from the ionisation current

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=I\,W/e\,,
\qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is actually in the beam.  The mass of air which is
instantaneously in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272
       \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air-kerma rate on the axis of the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}
          \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
          \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}=0.328\,
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the same rate is the tissue kerma rate in
the beam.

A surface element which is translated past the stationary $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm
mm}$ slit stays in the beam for the geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{e}}\simeq0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma deposited in one mono-energetic transit is

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{air}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\simeq 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\,{\rm ms}
\end{equation*}

of essentially air kerma $=$ tissue kerma at the surface for a single 
33 keV exposure.  The conventional dual-energy implementation is
$K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}\simeq8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
per resolved surface element, the $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
20 ms dwell normally quoted for synchrotron Laue micro-beam imaging.

The $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the crystal is
demagnified to the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus.  The number of essentially
independent energy channels which can be sampled on a given surface
element during the raster is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}\simeq\frac{50.0}{0.30}\simeq1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma integrated over the whole Laue band which is sampled
on a given point is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\,K_{M}\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted, i.e. $\sim0.6\;{\rm
mGy}$ surface exposure for the complete multiple-energy scan.  This is
$N_{E}$ times the $\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per mono-energetic 20 ms dwell,
the $ \approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\approx8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ dual-
energy peak surface kerma being the customary per-projection figure.
On a per-projection basis the familiar
$\dot K\simeq0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ at the focus, $K_{1}\simeq3.9\times10^{-6}\,
{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell, is obtained. ∎

10.text:
In air the ionisation power which is seen in the focus is

* $P_{a}=I\,W/e$ , $W/e\simeq 33.97\,{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is actually in the beam.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

Hence the air kerma rate on the axis of the $0.3\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{P_{a}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
      \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
      \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

and with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at $33\;{\rm keV}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\simeq2\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in soft tissue in the beam.  The $1\,{\rm pA}\simeq 0.10\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
at the focus is the familiar synchrotron micro-beam number.

A material point which is translated through the stationary focus stays
in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ slit for the geometrical dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}\simeq0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the quantity quoted in the statement.  The kerma accumulated in one
mono-energetic passage through the focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \simeq3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

in free air and, to the accuracy $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, in soft tissue.  With $W/e=33.97\;{\rm V}$

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \frac{I\,W}{e\,m_{a}}\,t_{d}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell, i.e. $\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak
air/tissue kerma at the focus reduced to the $\mu{\rm Gy}$ level by the
$20\;{\rm ms}$ residence.  This is the conventionally quoted
synchrotron Laue micro-beam surface dose.  For the usual two-energy
material-decomposition acquisition

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, essentially
negligible compared with diagnostic radiology.  The $E\,\mu_{en}/\rho$
factors cancel in the $I\to K$ conversion so that the $0.19\;{\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}$ kerma rate is independent of the quoted $0.328\;{\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$.

Remark on the Laue fan.  The $50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination at
the crystal is demagnified to the $0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focal spot, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}\simeq\frac{50.0}{0.30}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

essentially independent energy positions are contained in the fan.
Summed over the whole fan the kerma which can be deposited in a point
which is sampled over the complete energy range is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete $50\;{\rm mm}\times6\;{\rm mm}$ multi-energy raster, the
often quoted $\sim0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ cumulative surface kerma for the
full Laue band.  The $ \approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ mono-
energetic dwell, $\approx8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional dual-energy
set, is the per-projection surface dose which is normally quoted for
the $33\;{\rm keV}$ bent-Laue multiple-energy micro-imaging system. ∎

11.text:
In air the ionisation current is the power which is put into the air
which is seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,\frac{W}{e}\qquad \frac{W}{e}\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\;{\rm V}
          \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  The air which is seen at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}},\qquad 
V_{f}=A_{f}L ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}{\rm cm^{3}},\qquad 
m_{f}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx
3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

Hence the air kerma rate on the axis of the $0.30\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{f}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{f}}\approx
\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}\approx
1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With  $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the tissue kerma rate in the beam.  The fluence rate which follows
from $\dot K_{a}= \Phi E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ is
$\Phi\sim10^{8}{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ at 33 keV.

A material point stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ slit for the
geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\equiv
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}\approx2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the number quoted for the scan.  The surface kerma acquired in one
mono-energetic transit of the point through the focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $K_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20
{\rm ms}$ per 33 keV projection in free air and, to the accuracy
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, the same $\approx4\;\mu
{\rm Gy}$ surface kerma in soft tissue.  This is the customary
$\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak air/tissue kerma rate at the
$0.30\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ Laue focus, $ \approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
for the usual two-energy dual-energy acquisition, $2K_{1}\approx
7.7\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}$.

The $50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the crystal is
demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at the focus.  The number of
essentially independent energy positions which can be accessed in one
scan is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma summed over the whole Laue fan which is traversed by a
given surface element is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\approx N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
      {\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7
      {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete multiple-energy scan line, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the
numbers quoted, i.e. $\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy step,
$\approx7.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional dual-energy set, the
$\sim10^{-6}\!-\!10^{-5}\,$Gy level normally quoted for synchrotron
Laue multiple-energy micro-beam imaging.  The $K_{1}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
per 20 ms dwell is the peak per-energy surface dose; the $\sim0.6\;{\rm
mGy}$ value is the dose integrated over the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm
mm}$ monochromator field, $N_{E}\approx167$ times the single-energy
dwell dose. ∎

public_text.3:
In an air chamber the charge collected per unit mass is the air exposure.
With $W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=I\,\frac{W}{e},\qquad 
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{abs}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{air}A_{f}L}
\end{equation*}

is the air kerma rate in the air which is actually in the beam at the
focus.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}
        \times0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
        =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
           \approx6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

so that the peak air kerma rate on the axis of the $0.30\times6\;{\rm
mm^{2}}$ focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
           \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}=0.328\;
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at $33\;{\rm keV}$ the same photon fluence gives

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

as the tissue kerma rate in free space in the beam.

A material point which is carried through the stationary focus stays in
the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal slit for the geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{exp}}
      \approx2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the number quoted in the statement.  The kerma accumulated in one
mono-energetic passage is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.004\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per 33 keV exposure per surface element, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
peak in the beam.  This is the $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection
normally quoted for the synchrotron Laue micro-beam.  For the usual
dual-energy material decomposition

\begin{equation*}
K_{2}\simeq2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, $ \sim10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}$ of essentially negligible
surface exposure.

In terms of the monochromator geometry the $50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal
illumination at the crystal is demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at
the focus so that the number of essentially independent Laue energies
which can be put into the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\;,
\end{equation*}

the vertical size being unchanged.  The integrated surface kerma of a
point which is sampled over the whole Laue band is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete multi-energy scan of the $50\;{\rm mm}\times6\;{\rm mm}$
field, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted.  The $K_{1}\approx
3\!-\!4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell is the conventional per-
energy, per-resolution-element surface dose; the $N_{E}K_{1}\sim0.6\;{\rm
mGy}$ figure is the cumulative exposure obtained when the whole
$50{\rm mm}$ Laue fan is integrated.  The $W/e$ conversion makes the
result independent of the quoted $\mu_{en}/\rho$, which is used only to
justify $\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}$. ∎

public_text.12:
The air kerma in the illuminated part of the chamber is obtained from the
ionisation current

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=I\,W/e\;, \qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm J\,C^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air that is actually in the beam at the focus.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =0.03\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\,{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=\rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
2.72\times10^{-1}{\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that on the beam axis

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{abs}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}
           \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
           \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\dot K_{air}\simeq0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma at the
$0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the same rate is the tissue kerma rate in free
space, $\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}$.

A surface element which is translated through the stationary focus stays
in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal slit for the geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{exp}}\approx0.02\ {\rm s}\;,
\end{equation*}

the quantity quoted in the statement.  The kerma received in one
mono-energetic passage is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV dwell at the surface, $\approx
0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous in the focus.  In the usual
dual-energy implementation

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, the familiar $\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy,
$\sim10^{-5}{\rm Gy}$ per dual-energy image, peak surface kerma.

The $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the Laue crystal is
demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ at the focus, so that the number
of essentially independent energy channels in the Laue fan is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}\simeq\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\; .
\end{equation*}

The dose which is summed over the whole fan for one surface point is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\,{\rm mGy}$ surface kerma per complete
multiple-energy scan, $\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted,
i.e. $\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak at the focus reduced to the
$\sim4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ mono-energetic dwell, $\sim
7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional two-energy acquisition.  The
$\mu_{en}/\rho$ value cancels in the $I\to K$ conversion under electronic
equilibrium so the result is independent of the quoted $0.328\,{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
to first order. ∎

public_text.15:
The air kerma which the chamber sees in the $0.30\times 6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus is
obtained from the ionisation current

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=I\,W/e\,,
\qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{abs}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is actually in the beam.  The mass of air which is
instantaneously in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272
       \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air-kerma rate on the axis of the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}
          \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
          \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}=0.328\,
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV the same rate is the tissue kerma rate in
the beam.

A surface element which is translated past the stationary $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm
mm}$ slit stays in the beam for the geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t_{e}}\simeq0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma deposited in one mono-energetic transit is

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{air}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\simeq 3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\,{\rm ms}
\end{equation*}

of essentially air kerma $=$ tissue kerma at the surface for a single 
33 keV exposure.  The conventional dual-energy implementation is
$K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\,{\rm Gy}\simeq8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
per resolved surface element, the $\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
20 ms dwell normally quoted for synchrotron Laue micro-beam imaging.

The $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the crystal is
demagnified to the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus.  The number of essentially
independent energy channels which can be sampled on a given surface
element during the raster is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}\simeq\frac{50.0}{0.30}\simeq1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma integrated over the whole Laue band which is sampled
on a given point is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\,K_{M}\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with the numbers quoted, i.e. $\sim0.6\;{\rm
mGy}$ surface exposure for the complete multiple-energy scan.  This is
$N_{E}$ times the $\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per mono-energetic 20 ms dwell,
the $ \approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\approx8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ dual-
energy peak surface kerma being the customary per-projection figure.
On a per-projection basis the familiar
$\dot K\simeq0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ at the focus, $K_{1}\simeq3.9\times10^{-6}\,
{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms dwell, is obtained. ∎

public_text.10:
In air the ionisation power which is seen in the focus is

* $P_{a}=I\,W/e$ , $W/e\simeq 33.97\,{\rm J\,C^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is actually in the beam.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

Hence the air kerma rate on the axis of the $0.3\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{P_{a}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
      \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
      \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
      \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

and with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at $33\;{\rm keV}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\simeq2\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in soft tissue in the beam.  The $1\,{\rm pA}\simeq 0.10\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
at the focus is the familiar synchrotron micro-beam number.

A material point which is translated through the stationary focus stays
in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ slit for the geometrical dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/t_{e}}\simeq0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the quantity quoted in the statement.  The kerma accumulated in one
mono-energetic passage through the focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
      \simeq3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

in free air and, to the accuracy $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, in soft tissue.  With $W/e=33.97\;{\rm V}$

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \frac{I\,W}{e\,m_{a}}\,t_{d}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell, i.e. $\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak
air/tissue kerma at the focus reduced to the $\mu{\rm Gy}$ level by the
$20\;{\rm ms}$ residence.  This is the conventionally quoted
synchrotron Laue micro-beam surface dose.  For the usual two-energy
material-decomposition acquisition

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, essentially
negligible compared with diagnostic radiology.  The $E\,\mu_{en}/\rho$
factors cancel in the $I\to K$ conversion so that the $0.19\;{\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}$ kerma rate is independent of the quoted $0.328\;{\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$.

Remark on the Laue fan.  The $50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination at
the crystal is demagnified to the $0.30\;{\rm mm}$ focal spot, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}\simeq\frac{50.0}{0.30}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

essentially independent energy positions are contained in the fan.
Summed over the whole fan the kerma which can be deposited in a point
which is sampled over the complete energy range is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete $50\;{\rm mm}\times6\;{\rm mm}$ multi-energy raster, the
often quoted $\sim0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ cumulative surface kerma for the
full Laue band.  The $ \approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\;{\rm ms}$ mono-
energetic dwell, $\approx8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional dual-energy
set, is the per-projection surface dose which is normally quoted for
the $33\;{\rm keV}$ bent-Laue multiple-energy micro-imaging system. ∎

