$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, the same $\approx4\;\mu
{\rm Gy}$ surface kerma in soft tissue.  This is the customary
$\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak air/tissue kerma rate at the
$0.30\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ Laue focus, $ \approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
for the usual two-energy dual-energy acquisition, $2K_{1}\approx
7.7\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}$.

The $50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the crystal is
demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at the focus.  The number of
essentially independent energy positions which can be accessed in one
scan is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{c}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the kerma summed over the whole Laue fan which is traversed by a
given surface element is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\approx N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
      {\rm Gy}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7
      {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete multiple-energy scan line, $\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ with the
numbers quoted, i.e. $\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy step,
$\approx7.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a conventional dual-energy set, the
$\sim10^{-6}\!-\!10^{-5}\,$Gy level normally quoted for synchrotron
Laue multiple-energy micro-beam imaging.  The $K_{1}\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
per 20 ms dwell is the peak per-energy surface dose; the $\sim0.6\;{\rm
mGy}$ value is the dose integrated over the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm
mm}$ monochromator field, $N_{E}\approx167$ times the single-energy
dwell dose. ∎

12.text:
The current measures the power which is put into the air which is
instantaneously in the $0.30\times 6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e ,\qquad W/e\simeq33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is actually illuminated at the focus.  With
$w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}=0.030\,{\rm cm}$ and $h_{f}=6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.60\,{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.030\times0.60=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L_{c}=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the air mass which sees the 2.0 pA at any instant.  Hence on axis at
the focus

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}\simeq
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}\simeq
1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the free-in-air tissue kerma rate in the $0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
micro-beam.

A surface element which is translated through the stationary focus
remains in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ slit for the geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\simeq0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

quoted for the scan.  The kerma accumulated in one mono-energetic
transit is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}=\dot K_{t}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per 33 keV exposure,

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\ {\rm ms}
\end{equation*}

free-in-air $\approx$ soft tissue surface kerma per resolution element
per energy.  This is the conventional $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per
projection synchrotron Laue micro-beam figure.  For the conventional
two-energy material decomposition

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per raster line, $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, the dose actually
received by a tissue element during a standard multiple-energy
acquisition.

The $50\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the bent Laue crystal is
demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ at the focus,

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq W_{c}/w_{f}=50.0/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

essentially independent energy positions in the Laue fan.  Summed over
the whole fan the peak kerma which can be built up on a point sampled
through the complete energy band is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ multiple-energy scan,
$\approx0.64\ {\rm mGy}$ with the nominal numbers, i.e. $N_{E}$ times
the $ \approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per mono-energetic 20 ms dwell.  The
$3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\approx7.8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ dual energy,
is the usual per-projection surface dose quoted for the system; the
$\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ value is the $W_{c}/w_{f}$ integrated exposure over
the full monochromator field.  The $10\times$ low $3.8\times10^{-7}\ {\rm Gy}$
estimates are the $A_{f}=0.18\,{\rm cm^{2}}$ i.e. $0.30\,{\rm cm}$
instead of $0.030\,{\rm cm}$ mm$\to$cm conversion slip. ∎

