6.text:
In the $w_{f}\times h_{f}$ spot the ionising power which is put into the air
that is instantaneously in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e\qquad W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is instantaneously in the beam

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =0.030\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\simeq3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg},
\end{equation*}

is the air mass which sees the 2.0 pA at any instant.  On axis

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \dot E_{a}/m_{a}=I\,W/e/m_{a}
       \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
       \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air in the $0.30\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ Laue micro-beam.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at
33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the free-in-air, $\simeq$ soft-tissue, peak kerma rate in the focus.
The $\mu_{en}/\rho$ cancels in the $I\to K$ conversion and is used only to
justify $\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}$.

A material point stays in the stationary $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ slit for the
geometrical dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\simeq0.02\ {\rm s},
\end{equation*}

so that for one mono-energetic transit of one $0.30\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
resolution element

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}=\dot K_{t}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per 33 keV, $0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\;{\rm ms}$, in air $\approx$
tissue.  This is the conventional $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection
synchrotron Laue micro-beam surface kerma.  

For the conventional material-decomposition implementation

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq N_{E}K_{1},\qquad N_{E}=2,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\approx0.008\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per rastered surface element, $K_{1}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy,
is the normally quoted per-point multiple-energy surface kerma.

The $50\;{\rm mm}$ horizontal illumination of the bent Laue crystal is
demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at the focus.  The number of
essentially independent horizontal positions/energy channels in the Laue
fan which is used to build a $50\,{\rm mm}$ line is

\begin{equation*}
N_{fan}\simeq W_{c}/w_{f}=50.0/0.30\simeq1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the field-integrated peak kerma which can be built up on a point
sampled through the complete energy band, i.e. the $W_{c}/w_{f}$ integrated
cumulative surface exposure delivered by the bent-crystal multiple-energy
system over the $W_{c}\times h=50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ monochromator
field, is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{fan}K_{1}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\simeq0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ on the nominal numbers, $N_{fan}$ times the
$3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per-energy $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell.  The mean kerma
averaged over the 50 mm line is $K_{av}=K_{1}w_{f}/W_{c}\sim2\!-\!3\times
10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ per projection, $\sim70\!-\!80\;{\rm nGy}$ mean per
energy.  The $3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per-energy $20\;{\rm ms}$ dwell,
$\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ dual-energy per resolution element, is the
$0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak rate reduced to the conventional
$\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per-projection Laue micro-beam surface kerma; the
$\sim6\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\sim0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ $W_{c}/w_{f}$ value is the
field-integrated cumulative surface exposure over the full 50 mm Laue
fan.  The $10\times$ low $3.8\times10^{-7}\ {\rm Gy}$ estimates are the
$A_{f}=0.18\;{\rm cm^{2}}$ $0.30\,{\rm cm}$ instead of $0.030\,{\rm cm}$
mm$\to$cm slip. ∎

20.text:
In free air the ionisation current is a measure of the power which is
deposited in the air which is instantaneously in the $w_{f}\times h_{f}$
micro beam

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e\;\;\;\;W/e\simeq33.97{\rm \;V}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97\simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is in the beam at any instant.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =0.030\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}=1.8\times10^{-2}\ {\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}\ {\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272
     \simeq3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg},
\end{equation*}

so that on axis

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
         \simeq\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
         \simeq1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
         \simeq0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air in the $0.30\times6~{\rm mm^{2}}$ Laue micro-beam.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\simeq1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the free-in-air, $\simeq$ soft-tissue, peak kerma rate in the
micro-beam. The $\mu_{en}/\rho$ cancels in the $I\to K$ conversion.

A surface element stays in the stationary $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ slit for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\simeq0.02\;{\rm s} ,
\end{equation*}

so that the mono-energetic kerma for one resolution element is

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}=\dot K_{t}t_{d}\simeq1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \simeq3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times20\,{\rm ms}$, the familiar
$\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection synchrotron Laue surface kerma.  In
air $\approx$ tissue at 33 keV.

For the conventional material-decomposition implementation

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq N_{E}K_{1},\qquad N_{E}=2,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
K_{DE}\simeq2K_{1}\simeq7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq
7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.008\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per rastered $0.30\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ surface element,
$K_{1}\simeq3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, is the normally quoted
per-point, per-projection multiple-energy surface dose.

The 50 mm horizontal illumination of the bent Laue crystal is
demagnified to $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ at the focus. The number of
essentially independent horizontal energy/beam positions in the Laue
fan that makes up the 50 mm line is

\begin{equation*}
N_{f}\simeq W_{c}/w_{f}=50.0/0.30\simeq1.67\times10^{2} ,
\end{equation*}

so that summed over the whole $W_{c}\times h=50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
monochromator field the peak kerma which can be built up on a point
sampled through the complete energy band, i.e. the $W_{c}/w_{f}$-
integrated cumulative surface exposure delivered by the bent-crystal
multiple-energy system, is

\begin{equation*}
K_{M}\simeq N_{f}K_{1}\simeq1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \simeq6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\simeq0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy},
\end{equation*}

$\simeq0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ on the nominal numbers, $N_{f}$ times the
$3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per-energy 20 ms dwell.  The field averaged mean over
the 50 mm line is $K_{av}=K_{1}w_{f}/W_{c}\sim2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm
Gy}$ per projection, $\sim70\!-\!80\;{\rm nGy}$ mean per energy.  The
$3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per mono-energetic 20 ms dwell, $\approx7\!-\!8\;
\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the conventional $N_{E}=2$ dual-energy acquisition, is
the per-resolution-element peak; the $N_{f}K_{1}\sim6\times10^{-4}{\rm
Gy}\sim0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ value is the $W_{c}/w_{f}$ integrated cumulative
surface kerma normally quoted for the bent-crystal multiple-energy
$50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ scan.  This is the $\sim0.19\,{\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak rate reduced to $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy
20 ms dwell and to $\sim0.6\,{\rm mGy}$ when the $\approx167$-fold Laue
fan is summed. ∎

